1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,427
Mingalaba. This is Liam, in for Bear, with a short film for INFO seventy-three

2
00:00:04,447 --> 00:00:08,557
seventy-five, Branding and AI. You might think a polished portfolio proves what you

3
00:00:08,577 --> 00:00:12,687
can do. It only proves something if it cost you something to make.

4
00:00:12,757 --> 00:00:17,319
Three terms. A signal: something you show that stands in for what others can't see.

5
00:00:17,339 --> 00:00:21,900
A costly signal: one that's hard to fake cheaply, so it tells people apart. And

6
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:25,871
pooling: when everyone can produce the signal, so it stops telling anyone apart.

7
00:00:25,941 --> 00:00:31,034
In 1973, the economist Michael Spence asked a simple question. An employer wants

8
00:00:31,054 --> 00:00:36,540
productive people, but you can't see productivity until after the hire. So how does

9
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:37,327
anyone choose?

10
00:00:37,397 --> 00:00:42,150
They look at signals: things you can show that stand in for what they can't see.

11
00:00:42,170 --> 00:00:43,642
Spence's example was a degree.

12
00:00:43,712 --> 00:00:48,334
A degree works as a signal because it's costly, and costlier for some people than

13
00:00:48,354 --> 00:00:52,047
for others. It's hard to fake cheaply, so it tells people apart.

14
00:00:52,117 --> 00:00:56,442
When the cost holds, the signal separates people. Spence called that a separating

15
00:00:56,462 --> 00:01:01,121
equilibrium. The work later shared a Nobel prize, for markets where one side knows

16
00:01:01,141 --> 00:01:02,458
more than the other.

17
00:01:02,528 --> 00:01:07,985
Now AI has made some of those signals nearly free. A working demo app. A clean cover

18
00:01:08,005 --> 00:01:12,496
letter. A polished essay, a logo, a slide deck. In one experiment, programmers with

19
00:01:12,516 --> 00:01:17,007
an AI assistant built a small web server in less than half the time.

20
00:01:17,077 --> 00:01:21,384
When everyone can produce the signal, it stops sorting. Spence called that pooling.

21
00:01:21,404 --> 00:01:25,711
Everyone looks the same on paper, and the employer is back to guessing.

22
00:01:25,781 --> 00:01:29,503
So what didn't get cheap? Judgment: choosing what's worth making. Ideation: finding

23
00:01:29,523 --> 00:01:33,557
a problem real people actually have. Creative taste. And the effort of putting

24
00:01:33,577 --> 00:01:38,235
something in front of real users and changing it because of what they said. Those

25
00:01:38,255 --> 00:01:40,730
still cost you something, so they still signal.

26
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:45,385
So show the expensive part. The problem you chose, and why. The versions you threw

27
00:01:45,405 --> 00:01:50,297
away. What real users told you, and what you changed. The artifact is cheap now. The

28
00:01:50,317 --> 00:01:51,525
reasons behind it aren't.

29
00:01:51,595 --> 00:01:55,547
Your turn. Pick one project you'd show an employer, and paste this into Claude:

30
00:01:55,567 --> 00:01:59,803
Here's a project in my portfolio: describe it. Act as a skeptical hiring manager who

31
00:01:59,823 --> 00:02:04,343
knows AI can produce code and text cheaply. Tell me which parts of this project an

32
00:02:04,363 --> 00:02:08,599
AI could have made in an afternoon, and which parts show judgment an AI couldn't

33
00:02:08,619 --> 00:02:13,138
supply. Then suggest three things I could add so the costly part is visible to a

34
00:02:13,158 --> 00:02:16,827
stranger. Then check two things yourself. Is each costly part something you actually

35
00:02:16,847 --> 00:02:19,664
did? And could a stranger see it without asking you?

36
00:02:19,734 --> 00:02:24,205
Cheap to Make, Cheap to Signal. At Nik Bear Brown.
